CRYPTO-HELICAL
Gogiids, in general, have
three morphological attributes common among echinoderms:
brachioles for feeding, a main body chamber for internal organs,
and a holdfast for attachment to the substratum (Fig. 1).

5.2. Issues on Gogiida
The stalk is an important morphologic feature for the Order
Gogiida (Parsley and Prokop, 2004). The most recent reviews
(Parsley and Zhao, 2006, 2012; Zhao et al., 2008) of the
Family Eocrinidae included Gogia, Sinoeocrinus, Acanthocys-
tites, Akadocrinus, Guizhoueocrinus, and Globoeocrinus, but
excluded Balangicystis. Recently, Balangicystis has been reas-
signed to the Family Lyracystidae (Sprinkle et al., 2011).
The Eocrinidae is characterized by a polyplated stalk, a
crypto-helically plated theca with epispires, and unbranched
brachioles (Fig. 3). However, Akadocrinus bears a columnal-
like stalk (Fatka and Kordule, 1991) that is different from the
other gogiids mentioned above. The closest stalked family is
the Lyracystidae Sprinkle and Collins, 2006, which is similar
to Eocrinidae by bearing similar stalk and thecal plates but is
different in having a very long stalk, a decrease in number of
thecal plates, and the unusual endotomous branching brachioles
(Sprinkle et al., 2011). In contrast, Lichenoididae Jaekel (see
Parsley and Prokop, 2004) is different from both gogiid families
due to the lack of a stalk and a theca with highly reduced thecal
plates. Phylogenetic relationships among gogiid families cannot
be fully understood without a complete treatment of taxonomic
revision of the Order Gogiida with cladistic analyses, but such
task is beyond the scope of current work.


